# Copy to .env — loaded automatically by `npm run dev` (dotenv in vite.config.ts).
#
# With no .env (or everything below unset), the example runs in dev mode:
# sign-in emails and SMS codes are printed to the server console instead of
# being sent.

# --- Email (magic link + code) ---
#
# Setting SMTP_HOST switches to sending real email.
# Ready-made configs — copy one instead of this file:
#
#   cp .env.example.mailpit .env   # see emails in a local Mailpit web inbox
#   cp .env.example.smtp .env      # send real email via an SMTP provider
#
#SMTP_HOST=
#SMTP_PORT=
#SMTP_USER=
#SMTP_PASS=
#EMAIL_FROM=

# --- SMS: pick ONE of the two Twilio setups below ---
#
# Both text the user a sign-in code and look identical to them. They differ
# in who owns the code and what it takes to start:
#
#   Twilio Verify      quick to set up, cheaper at low volume.
#                      Twilio generates, texts, and checks the code using
#                      senders it already registered. Nothing to provision,
#                      nothing to wait on. $0.05 per successful
#                      verification plus the channel fee.
#
#   Twilio Messaging   slow to set up, cheaper at high volume.
#                      You own the number and this app owns the code.
#                      ~$0.011-0.013 per US SMS, but US traffic first needs
#                      A2P 10DLC brand and campaign registration: days to
#                      weeks of approval plus monthly fees per campaign and
#                      per number. Until it clears, carriers filter the
#                      texts while the API still reports success.
#
# Those fixed monthly fees are what flips the answer: at a few hundred
# sign-ins a month or fewer they usually exceed the whole Verify bill.
# Choose Messaging when volume is high enough to amortize them, or when you
# want RCS or control over the message text. (Prices are Twilio's US list
# rates at the time of writing; check their pricing page.)
#
# Setting a full set of vars for either switches from console-printed codes
# to real texts; if any are missing the app falls back to the console
# transport and logs which ones are absent. Verify takes precedence when
# both are configured.

# --- SMS via Twilio Verify (quick setup, cheaper at low volume) ---
#
# Account SID and auth token — console home page, under Account Info:
#   https://console.twilio.com
#
# Verify service SID (starts with VA) — Develop > Verify > Services, then
# "Create new". The friendly name you give the service is what appears in
# the text the user receives, so name it after your app:
#   https://console.twilio.com/us1/develop/verify/services
#
#TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=ACxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
#TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=your-auth-token
#TWILIO_VERIFY_SERVICE_SID=VAxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
#
# Per-attempt outcomes (approved, wrong code, expired) are in the Verify log:
#   https://console.twilio.com/us1/monitor/logs/verify

# --- SMS via Twilio Messaging (slow setup, cheaper at high volume) ---
#
# Same account SID and auth token as above (https://console.twilio.com),
# plus a sender: provide TWILIO_SMS_MESSAGING_SERVICE_SID (preferred;
# required for RCS) from Develop > Messaging > Services, or TWILIO_SMS_FROM
# (a Twilio number you own, in E.164) from Develop > Phone Numbers.
#
#TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=ACxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
#TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=your-auth-token
#TWILIO_SMS_MESSAGING_SERVICE_SID=MGxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
#TWILIO_SMS_FROM=+15005550006
#
# Text never arrives? Check the delivery log (carriers can filter messages
# the API accepted, e.g. 30034 = unregistered A2P 10DLC number):
#   https://console.twilio.com/us1/monitor/logs/sms

# Session signing secret. A hardcoded dev-only default is used when unset.
#JWT_SECRET=
